  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV3">
      <l>poruppē vemparal puṟavoṭu paḻaṉam</l>
<l>parappamai vāri kuṟuñcimutaṟ pākē.<anchor xml:id="pake"/></l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
<l>Mountains, hot stones, forest, farmland, and waters joined to the expanse<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">parappu</hi></q> literally means ‘expanse’ but I understand it here as an elliptical reference to the sea or ocean. Following the commentary, I understand that this basic place-element refers to the area around the backwaters, river-mouths, and the sea. Theoretically, there is another way to construe <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">parappu amai vāri</hi></q>. If one assumes the second layer of the commentary is not supplying anything in its gloss, then one would expect <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">parappu amai</hi></q> to mean ‘great sand dunes’ (<q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">peru maṇal ekkar</hi></q>), which would also mirror the description found in the TP (cited in the apparatus). This would require <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">amai-ttal</foreign> (‘to produce’), instead of the weak root. Quite how one gets from there to ‘products of the sea’, i.e., sand dunes, is not so obvious.</note></l>
<l>are the allotments [of the five poetic settings], beginning with <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṟuñci</hi></term>.<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">This non-standard spelling has been retained as it seems to be part of a wider pattern in this manuscript where words commonly spelled with an <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">i</foreign> are written with a <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">u</foreign>. This may reflect the local dialect of the place where this text was composed or where the manuscripts were copied.</note></l></lg>

</div>
</body>
</text>
<text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV3comm1">eṉpatu vain tiṇaikkum iṭamutal ām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.</p>
<p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make 
known the way in which the basic place-element<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">The <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> shows that this refers to the dominant geographical feature of each poetic setting.</note> of all of the five 
poetic settings occurs.</p></div>
<div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV3comm2">malaic cāralum vem paral attamum ciṟu tūṟuṭaip puṟavumalar kañaliya 
paḻaṉamuñ cūḻnta vayaluṅ kaṭaloṭu kaḻi cārnta peru maṇal ekkaruṅ kuṟuñci pālai 
 mullai marutaneytal eṉa vaim perun tiṇaiyunilamutal eṉṟa vāṟu. <num>3.</num></p>
<p xml:lang="en">The mountainside, the road with hot stones, the forest with small bushes, the paddy-fields
 surrounded by water-tanks dense with blossoms,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">I understand the <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">-um</hi></q> 
 particle on <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">paḻaṉamum</hi></q> to have a distributive function. One could, however, also make sense of this as a co-ordinative particle. In that case this phrase would read: ‘the water-tanks 
 full of blossoms and the surrounding paddy-fields’.</note> and the great sand dunes joined to the backwaters and the sea are the basic place-elements<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">Note that the commentary here uses different
  terminology from that in the verse – <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">nilamutal</hi></q> (<foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">nilam mutal</foreign>). This is the same as what we find 
  in TP 4n.</note> of all the five great poetic settings<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">The designation 
  <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">aim perun tiṇai</hi></q> (‘five great poetic settings’) is odd. I think the author supplied 
  the qualifier <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">perum</hi></q> to make clear the difference between the five mentioned here and 
  the other two <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">tiṇai</hi></term>-s – <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaikkiḷai</hi></term> and 
  <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">peruntiṇai</hi></term> – the settings in which poetry about unsuitable love occurs. 
  Cf. TP 1n for a mention of seven <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">akattiṇai</hi></term>.</note> called 
  <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṟuñci</hi></term>, <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">pālai</hi></term>, <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mullai</hi></term>, <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">marutam</hi></term>, and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">neytal</hi></term>.</p>
 </div>
 </body>
</text><standOff corresp="#TNV3" type="wordsplit">
