<standOff type="notes4" corresp="#TNV3"><note xml:lang="en" target="#pake">Tolkāppiyar provides these as the defining geographic characteristics of the poetic settings, omitting <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">pālai</hi></term>, which is a ‘situational desert’ (as mountains and forests can turn into desert in summer; Takahashi 1995, 55), in TP 5n (see the apparatus notes to TNV 7 for the full <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>): <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaṭuṟai yulakam</hi></q> (‘the region with forests’) for <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mullai</hi></term>; <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">maivarai yulakam</hi></q> (‘the region with dark mountains’) for <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṟiñci</hi></term>; <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">tīmpuṉal ulakam</hi></q> (‘the region with sweet floods’) for <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">marutam</hi></term>; and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">perumaṇal ulakam</hi></q> (‘the region with great sand [dunes]’) for <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">neytal</hi></term>.</note></standOff>
<standOff type="notes4" corresp="#TNV3"><note xml:lang="en" target="#pake">Likewise, Nakkīrar provides a different list of characteristics (IAC 1, p. 23–24): <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">malaiyum malaicārnta iṭamum</hi></q> (‘mountains and their adjoining area’) for <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṟiñci</hi></term>; <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaṭalum kaṭalcārnta iṭamum</hi></q> (‘the sea and its adjoining area’) for <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">neytal</hi></term>; <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kāṭum kāṭucārnta iṭamum</hi></q> (‘forests and their adjoining area’) for <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mullai</hi></term>; and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">paḻaṉamum paḻaṉañ cārnta iṭamum</hi></q> (‘farmland and its adjoining area’) for <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">marutam</hi></term>.</note></standOff>
